      "method": "v1.1.0 case-law rebuild: authorities selected per decision point, each opinion retrieved from CourtListener and read; holding and quote taken from the opinion text; independently re-verified before release. Previous keyword-matched Justia listings were removed.",
      "verified_date": "2026-09-21",
      "key_cases": [
        {
          "case_name": "Suter v. Artist M.",
          "citation": "503 U.S. 347 (1992)",
          "court": "Supreme Court of the United States",
          "year": 1992,
          "date_decided": "1992-03-25",
          "holding": "The Supreme Court held that the Adoption Assistance and Child Welfare Act's reasonable efforts requirement creates no privately enforceable right, so beneficiaries cannot sue to enforce it under the Act itself or under 42 U.S.C. 1983.",
          "relevance": "It is why CFSR findings and program improvement plans are the operative accountability tool rather than court enforcement by affected families.",
          "quote": "We hold that the Act does not create an enforceable right on behalf of respondents.",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/112712/suter-v-artist-m/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 112712
        },
        {
          "case_name": "People ex rel. J.S.B., Jr.",
          "citation": "2005 S.D. 3, 691 N.W.2d 611",
          "court": "South Dakota Supreme Court",
          "year": 2005,
          "date_decided": "2005-01-05",
          "holding": "The court held that ASFA's aggravated-circumstances exception to reasonable efforts does not override ICWA's active efforts requirement, but affirmed termination because DSS in fact continued to provide active efforts that proved unsuccessful.",
          "relevance": "It shows a state court policing federal child-welfare compliance directly, rather than deferring to the agency's reading of federal law.",
          "quote": "Under ICWA, DSS was bound by law to make \"active efforts\" to reunite J.S.B with his father, but it was not required to persist with futile efforts.",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/901424/the-people-of-the-state-of-south-dakota-in-the-interest-of-jsb-jr/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 901424
        },
        {
          "case_name": "Puffy's, LLC v. Department of Health",
          "citation": "2025 S.D. 10",
          "court": "South Dakota Supreme Court",
          "year": 2025,
          "date_decided": "2025-02-19",
          "holding": "The court affirmed a writ of mandamus compelling the Department of Health to issue a registration certificate, holding that the plain text of its own administrative rule obligated the agency to act and that the applicant had no other adequate remedy.",
          "relevance": "Performance obligations written into rule are judicially enforceable in South Dakota, which matters when federal review findings are translated into state rules.",
          "quote": "We conclude that the text of ARSD 44:90:03:16 obligated the Department to issue the registration certificate to Puffy's, and Puffy's had a clear legal right to receive it.",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/10803000/puffys-llc-v-dept-of-health/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 10336412
        }
      ],
